<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Choctaw Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1907 CE (approx.) · capital Tuskahoma</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest nation of the Mississippi region, long allied with France, and the first of the south-eastern peoples forced west on the Trail of Tears.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Choctaw formed from the merging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian</a>-descended communities in central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in the 16th and 17th centuries. Their tradition places their origin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanih-waiya">Nanih Waiya</a>, a great mound in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-county-mississippi">Winston County</a>. By around 1700 they lived in some 50 towns grouped in western, eastern and southern divisions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chiefs of the three divisions met in council, and matrilineal clans structured society. The Choctaw were skilled farmers and ballplayers. As the main allies of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-louisiana">French Louisiana</a> they fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chickasaw-nation">Chickasaw Nation</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/natchez-chiefdom">Natchez chiefdom</a>, though a pro-British faction under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-shoe">Red Shoe</a> sparked a civil war in 1747 to 1750. After 1783 they dealt with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushmataha">Pushmataha</a> led warriors against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sticks">Red Sticks</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creek-war">Creek War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-dancing-rabbit-creek">Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek</a> in 1830 the Choctaw ceded their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> and were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a> in 1831 to 1833. Their government, seated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuskahoma-oklahoma">Tuskahoma</a>, was dissolved by 1907 but later restored; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-band-of-choctaw-indians">Mississippi Band of Choctaw</a> stayed in the east.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/choctaw-nation.jpg" alt="Choctaw Nation" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, commissioned by the Choctaw Nation [3] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Choctaw_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1907 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tuskahoma</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Red Shoe, Pushmataha, Greenwood LeFlore</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Choctaw traditional religion, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Choctaw</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Choctaw, Chahta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=choctaw-nation" aria-label="Choctaw Nation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2224 945 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Choctaw Nation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2544 1201L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2544 1201h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2544 1201h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=choctaw-nation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
